WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
Moodle administration is the stewardship of a learning service, not just the ability to find settings.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:19.800
A well-operated Moodle LMS site connects learners, teachers, course managers, identity systems, email delivery, storage, scheduled processing, reporting, security, privacy, and support.

3
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.620
The administrator's work is to keep those parts aligned with the organization's learning purpose.

4
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:36.000
A site can load successfully and still be poorly governed if nobody owns roles, plugins, backups, incident response, or the identity data that drives enrolments.

5
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.600
It helps to separate responsibilities.

6
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:47.340
Site administration covers global settings, plugins, authentication, roles, reports, and operational policy.

7
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.880
Course administration manages individual course spaces.

8
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.660
Teaching is about learning design and learner support.

9
00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:01.720
Server administration covers the web stack, database platform, storage, mail routing, and monitoring.

10
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:08.460
Privacy and security administration add data handling, risk review, audit evidence, and incident readiness.

11
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.560
In a small organization, one person may wear several hats, but the responsibilities still need names.

12
00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.120
Full site-administrator access deserves special care.

13
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:27.020
The default Manager role can handle many course, user, grade, and delegated administrative tasks, depending on context and capabilities.

14
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.120
That does not make every manager a full site administrator.

15
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:42.660
The safest operating model uses least privilege, separates emergency access from routine work, and keeps a backup administrator ready without normalizing shared accounts or uncontrolled privilege.

16
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.140
The practical administrator maintains a living baseline.

17
00:01:46.240 --> 00:02:05.360
That record should include the Moodle version, hosting model, authentication methods, enrolment methods, custom roles, course categories, enabled plugins, themes, integrations, scheduled tasks, backup settings, privacy settings, security choices, and support contacts.

18
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.180
The baseline turns an inherited site from a mystery into an operated service.

19
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.220
Evidence matters, but evidence must be interpreted carefully.

20
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:28.080
System status, task status, logs, configuration changes, backup reports, restore tests, performance measurements, support trends, and security notices all help administrators decide what to do next.

21
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.260
A report existing is not the same as the process being healthy.

22
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.080
Logs do not prove learning occurred, and a completed backup job does not prove recovery will work.

23
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:58.080
This course treats Moodle LMS administration as a lifecycle: plan, install and configure, structure the site, manage identities and access, govern courses and plugins, operate scheduled services, monitor security and performance, protect data, test recovery, upgrade safely, and improve.

24
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.420
Moodle LMS is the baseline here, not Moodle Workplace.

25
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:11.320
The goal is continuous service stewardship: clear ownership, controlled change, least privilege, documented decisions, and recoverable operations.
